# Deployment & Testing Procedures

## Script-Based Deployment (Required)
- **Edit Scripts, Not Quadlet Files**: Always modify `podman-setup.sh` scripts and run them - quadlet files are auto-generated
- **Scripts Generate Quadlets**: Running `podman-setup.sh` automatically creates/updates `.container` quadlet files
- **Quadlet Files Are Generated**: Never edit `.container` files directly - they are output from scripts
- **Why This Approach?**: Scripts ensure consistent deployment, proper quadlet generation, and automatic systemd integration
- **Manual Commands Only For Debugging**: Use direct podman/systemctl commands only when scripts don't support operations

## Local Testing Workflow
- Deploy containers locally before server deployment
- Check port availability with `ss -tlnp | grep :PORT`
- Verify container health with `podman ps` and `podman logs <container>`
- Test NGINX config with `nginx -t` after configuration changes
- Verify reverse proxy routing works correctly
- **File Synchronization**: Copy local changes to server with `scp` before testing
- **Internal Port Verification**: Check if services are actually listening with `podman exec <container> ss -tlnp`
- **Nginx Config Generation**: The `generate-nginx-conf-file` command in podman-setup.sh creates correct nginx configurations

## Server Deployment Workflow
- **Repository Location**: SelfHost repository is at `~/selfhost/` on `legion@aevion`
- **File Transfer**: Use `scp <local-path> legion@aevion:~/selfhost/<remote-path>` for config updates
- **Service Restart**: Use `systemctl --user restart <service-name>` after config changes
- **Verification**: Check `systemctl --user status <service-name>` and `podman logs <container-name>`
- **Quadlet Updates**: Run `podman-setup.sh` scripts on server when changing container definitions
- **Systemd Reload**: Run `systemctl --user daemon-reload` after quadlet file changes

## Service Integration Testing
- **Check Container Logs First**: Always review `podman logs <container>` before testing endpoints to confirm service is running properly
  - Look for: successful initialization, "[ls.io-init] done.", service startup messages
  - Common issues: permission errors, missing dependencies, database connection failures
- **Endpoint Testing**: Test direct container ports before reverse proxy
- Test service accessibility via configured subdomains
- Verify SSL certificate functionality
- Check service interdependencies
- Validate environment variable loading
- Test service startup and shutdown procedures
- **LinuxServer Containers**: Always verify official documentation for version-specific changes
- **HTTPS Requirements**: Check for mandatory HTTPS in newer container versions

## Image Source Resolution
- **Verify Image Availability**: Check `podman search <image>` or Docker Hub for correct image names
- **Official vs Community Images**: Prefer official images, but use community images when official don't exist
- **LinuxServer Alternative**: When LinuxServer images aren't available, search Docker Hub for alternatives
- **Tag Selection**: Use `latest` for production, specific tags for stability

## Container Configuration
- **User Management**: Check official documentation for preferred user handling method
  - LinuxServer containers typically use PUID/PGID environment variables instead of `--user` flag
  - Community containers may require `--user` flag or have different user handling approaches
  - Always verify documentation before choosing between PUID/PGID vs `--user` flag
  - If documentation is unclear or conflicting, ask user for clarification on preferred method
- **Required Environment Variables**: Always check official docs for mandatory env vars (TZ, PUID, PGID)
- **Volume Permissions**: Containers handle their own permissions when using proper user management
- **Port Binding**: Use `127.0.0.1:port` for reverse proxy, avoid host networking unless required
- **Container Networking**: Understand how containers communicate
  - Containers in different network modes use different IP addressing
  - podman containers may use 10.0.2.2 as gateway to reach host services
  - Always verify container networking when configuring reverse proxy upstream IPs
- **Service-Specific Setup**: Some containers need special volume structures or initialization (e.g., book ingest folders)

## Common Deployment Tasks
- **Add New Service**: Follow the selfhost-service skill (`.cursor/skills/selfhost-service/SKILL.md`) or copy existing app structure
- **Update Images**: Use `scripts/update_images.sh` for container updates
- **Monitor Services**: Check via Grafana/Prometheus dashboards, Jellystat for media analytics
- **Network Changes**: Update Tailscale configurations as needed
- **Bulk Service Deploy**: Use `mask` tasks (see `maskfile.md`) for starting/stopping all services

## Service Management
- **All services run as systemd user services** (not system services)
- **Control commands**: `systemctl --user <start|stop|restart|status> <service-name>`
- **Log viewing**: `journalctl --user -u <service-name> --no-pager` or `podman logs <container-name>`
- **Rootless operation**: All containers run without root privileges for security

## Deployment Strategies
- **Reverse Proxy (Recommended)**: Use NGINX for SSL termination and security layer
- **Direct LAN Access**: Expose ports directly when SSL certificates unavailable
- **Port Binding**: Use `${LOCALHOST_IP}` for reverse proxy, remove binding for LAN access
- **Container Networking**: Use pasta (plain or with `--map-host-loopback,10.0.2.2`) — see `.cursor/rules/security-practices.mdc` for which variant each container needs. Only `node-exporter` uses `--network host`; slirp4netns is no longer used anywhere in the stack.

## Troubleshooting Steps
- **Logs**: `journalctl --user -u <service>` (systemd) or `podman logs <container>` (container)
- **Container Status**: `podman ps -a` to check container state, `systemctl --user status <service>` for systemd
- **Image Issues**: When `podman run` fails with 403 Forbidden, check Docker Hub for correct image name
- **Permission Errors**: Ensure proper user management based on container documentation (PUID/PGID vs `--user` flag)
- **Port Binding**: Verify ports are bound with `ss -tlnp | grep :PORT`, check container publish settings
- **Environment Variables**: Cross-reference with official container documentation for required variables
- **Metrics**: Prometheus/Grafana dashboards for system monitoring
- **Network Issues**: Check Tailscale status and DNS resolution
- **Performance**: Monitor with `podman stats` and system resources
- **Common Issues**: Port conflicts, permission issues, network connectivity, incorrect image sources
- **Container Version Changes**: Check LinuxServer documentation for breaking changes (e.g., HTTPS requirements)
- **Quadlet Issues**: Run `systemctl --user daemon-reload` after config changes

## Backup & Recovery
- **Container Data**: Persistent volumes backed up regularly
- **Configurations**: Version controlled configs backed up
- **Database Dumps**: Automated dumps for database services
- **Restore Process**: Documented procedures for service recovery